==English==
{{was wotd|2009|December|29}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*peth₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|impetuous}}, from {{der|en|fro|impetueux}}, from {{der|en|LL.|impetuōsus||violent}}, from {{der|en|la|impetus||attack, violence}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/imˈpɛt͡ʃuəs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-impetuous.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-impetuous-NEW.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Making [[arbitrary]] decisions, especially in an impulsive and forceful manner.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|author=John Weeks Moore|title=Complete Encyclopaedia of Music|chapter=[[s:Complete Encyclopaedia of Music/B/Beethoven, Louis Van|Beethoven, Louis Van]]
|passage=But it was natural, that the '''impetuous''', restless young artist should incline more to excess of strength than of delicacy in his playing.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Newcomes|passage=The shrewd kind confidant used gently to hint the sad fact to the '''impetuous''' hero of this piece. The '''impetuous''' hero knew this quite well.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Terry Jones|title=Poor Tony Blair wakes up|work=The Guardian|date=2003-03-16|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2003/mar/16/iraq21|issn=0261-3077|passage=The prime minister thought he could sagely steer his '''impetuous''' American friends away from actions they would later regret. It turns out they were just playing him for a patsy[.]}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/books/archive/2023/09/elon-musk-biography-book-walter-isaacson/675426/|title=We Don’t Need Another Antihero|work=The Atlantic|date=2023-09-24|author=Sarah Frier|passage=How these developments might come about and what they will mean for humanity seems far more important to probe than Isaacson’s preferred focus on explaining Musk’s abusive, erratic, '''impetuous''' behavior.}}
# Characterized by sudden [[violence]] or [[vehemence]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=IX|stanza=14|page=311|passage=For with ſuch puiſſance and '''impetuous''' maine / Thoſe Champions broke on them, that forſt the fly, / Like ſcattered Sheepe, whenas the Shepherds ſwaine / A Lyon and a Tigre doth eſpye, / With greedy pace forth ruſhing from the foreſt nye.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1794|author=w:Ann Radcliffe|title=w:The Mysteries of Udolpho|section=vol. II, [[s:The Mysteries of Udolpho/Volume II/Chapter I|chapter I]]|passage=He stands, and views in the faint rays<br>Far, far below, the torrent's rising surge,<br>And listens to the wild '''impetuous''' roar}}
#* {{RQ:Wollstonecraft Maria|chapter=1|passage=After being two days the prey of '''impetuous''', varying emotions, Maria began to reflect more calmly on her present situation, for she had actually been rendered incapable of sober reflection, by the discovery of the act of atrocity of which she was the victim.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=III|chapter=4|passage="Oh! where, where is my uncle?" cried Elizabeth, darting from her seat as she finished the letter, in eagerness to follow him, with-out losing a moment of the time so precious; but as she reached the door, it was opened by a servant, and Mr. Darcy appeared. Her pale face and '''impetuous''' manner made him start {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|passage=Rough and '''impetuous''' as a wild boar, where only earthly force was to be apprehended, he had all the characteristic terrors of a Saxon respecting fawns, forest-fiends, white women, and the whole of the superstitions which his ancestors had brought with them from the wilds of Germany.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Tramp Abroad|chapter=VI|passage=When they were placed in position they thought it was time to begin—and then did begin, too, and with a most '''impetuous''' energy, without waiting for anybody to give the word.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=250|passage=Having made a few vain attempts at engrossing my attention in my book, I was obliged to let myself be carried away by the '''impetuous''' torrent of the squire's eloquence.}}
#* '''1917''' rev. '''1925''', {{w|Ezra Pound}}, "Canto I"
#*: Unsheathed the narrow sword,
#*: I sat to keep off the '''impetuous''' impotent dead {{...}}

